#### Jet selection cuts

The jets reconstructed from tracks and towers are subject to selection cuts summarized in Table 3.4. For each jet a "detector pseudorapidity" \(\eta_{\rm detector}\) is calculated as pseudorapidity of the vector from the beamline at \(z=0\) to the intersection of the jet thrust axis with the BEMC8. A limit on \(\eta_{\rm detector}\) ensures that the jet, regardless of the vertex position, points within a region of appreciable BEMC and TPC acceptance. The physical jet rapidity (a rapidity of its thrust axis) is also limited to match a cut that will be imposed on particle jets in the definition of the cross section. The jets are also required to point close to the center of a jet patch that was above the trigger threshold. An important quantity of "transverse neutral energy fraction" \(R_{\rm T}\) is defined for a detector jet as:

Footnote 8: Assumed to be a cylinder with \(R=225\) cm

\[R_{\rm T}\equiv\frac{\sum p_{T;{\rm tower}}}{\sum p_{T;{\rm track}}+\sum p_{T;{ \rm tower}}} \tag{3.5}\]

We are generally looking for jets that are simultaneously observed by both the TPC and EMCs to avoid various backgrounds. Hence a cut on \(R_{\rm T}\) is placed to avoid the extremes of track-dominated or tower-dominated jets.

### Quality assurance

#### 3.5.1 L0 trigger

The radiation present during RHIC operations can cause bit flips in the BEMC electronics, which may result in excessive jet patch triggers fired, either by creating hot towers or by directly affecting the trigger electronics. The latter case can be partially rectified by reevaluating the trigger states in post-processing by software called a "trigger simulator" that implements the DSM board algorithms to operate on recorded tower ADC values. The hot towers can also be discovered individually, and by masking them out before applying the trigger simulator, we can filter out the extra triggered events again.

