### Unfolding the detector response

The particle level jet cross sections \(x_{j}^{\rm data}\) are obtained from the measured detector level cross sections \(b_{i}^{\rm data}\) according to the following relation:

\[x_{j}^{\rm data}=\sum_{i}\mathcal{M}_{ij}^{-1}\;b_{i}^{\rm data} \tag{6.7}\]

where the response matrix \(\mathcal{M}\) is given by

\[\mathcal{M}_{ij}=\left(\frac{b_{i}^{\rm simu.}}{\sum_{k}A_{ik}}\right)\left( \frac{A_{ij}}{x_{j}^{\rm simu.}}\right) \tag{6.8}\]

Three components are required to construct the response matrix: the particle level spectrum \(x_{j}^{\rm simu.}\), the simulation's detector level spectrum \(b^{\rm simu.}\) and the covariance of particle and detector spectra of matched jets \(A_{ij}\).

\[x_{j}^{\rm simu.}\equiv\sum_{\rm events}\omega_{\rm event}^{\rm simu.}\sum_{ \begin{subarray}{c}\text{particle level}\\ \text{jets in event}\end{subarray}}\Theta((\eta^{\rm jet},p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet }-\Delta p_{\rm T}^{\rm UE})\in\text{bin }j) \tag{6.9}\]

There are no particle level cuts other than the selection for specific \(\eta\hskip-3.0pt\cdot\hskip-3.0ptp_{\rm T}\) bins. The weight \(\omega_{\rm event}^{\rm simu.}\) is a product of the partonic \(p_{\rm T}\) bin weights (Section 4.1.2) with the fudge factors (Sec

