#### EMC calibration and response to hadrons

A standard formula for evaluating the uncertainty on the contribution of the BEMC to jet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) that was used for previous jet spin asymmetry analyses in STAR looks like (proof given in Appendix E):

\[\frac{\delta\{p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{jet}}\}}{p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{jet}}}= \sqrt{R_{\mathrm{T}}^{2}\left(\frac{\delta\{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower}}\}}{E _{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower}}}\right)^{2}+(1-R_{\mathrm{T}})^{2}\left(\frac{ \delta\{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{had.}}\}}{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{had.}}} \right)^{2}} \tag{6.20}\]

where the first contribution

\[\frac{\delta\{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower}}\}}{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower }}}\equiv\sqrt{\left(\frac{\delta\{\mathrm{gain}\}}{\mathrm{gain}}\right)^{2 }+\left(\frac{\delta\{\mathrm{tower\;eff.}\}}{\mathrm{tower\;eff.}}\right)^{2}} \tag{6.21}\]

scales with neutral energy and represents the uncertainty related to the calibration of the calorimeter's response to the EM-showers (that, for measurement of jets, are caused by \(\gamma\)'s from \(\pi^{0}\) decays).

The second contribution

\[\frac{\delta\{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{had.}}\}}{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{had.} }}\equiv\left(\frac{1}{\epsilon_{\mathrm{trk}}}S_{\mathrm{hadron}}-p_{\mathrm{ proj}}\right)\times f_{\mathrm{had.\,resp.}}\times\frac{\delta\{f_{\mathrm{had.\,resp.}}\}}{f_{ \mathrm{had.\,resp.}}} \tag{6.22}\]

scales with the charged transverse momentum fraction and corresponds to the uncertainty due to the response to hadronic showers. The formalism looks a bit more complicated this time, as it accounts for the hadronic subtraction procedure (described in Section 3.4.4) which partially reduces the uncertainty as indicated by subtraction of \(p_{\mathrm{proj}}\)3.

Footnote 3: Moreover, a better hadronic subtraction procedure that could subtract energy from clusters of towers instead of individual towers could increase the value of \(p_{\mathrm{proj}}\) to be even closer to 1.

The EEMC uncertainties are conservatively estimated to be about the same as the BEMC.

The values of the parameters for Eq. (6.21) and Eq. (6.22) are given in Table 6.5. The uncertainty for the 4 "outer" BEMC rings can be neglected as the majority of 36 rings are associated with the "inner" BEMC ring uncertainty. This evaluates to \(\frac{\delta\{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower}}\}_{\mathrm{calib.}}}{E_{\mathrm{ T}}^{\mathrm{tower}}}=3.2\%\)and \(\frac{\delta\{E_{\rm{had.}}^{\rm{had.}}\}}{E_{\rm{had.}}^{\rm{had.}}}=1.1\%\).

From Table 3.1 one can infer the value of a conversion factor from \(\mathrm{Ge\kern-1.0ptV}\) to DSM ADC units which is \(\approx 4.2\) (DSM ADC units)/\(\mathrm{Ge\kern-1.0ptV}\). A 3% EMC energy uncertainty for an average trigger threshold thus corresponds to about 1 DSM ADC unit. This uncertainty can be estimated by adjusting the Jet Patch trigger thresholds in the trigger simulator.

