#### 7.2.1 Fit parametrization

The standard parametrization of HERAPDF2.0 [82] is the following:

\[x\,g(x) =A_{g}\,x^{B_{g}}(1-x)^{C_{g}}-A^{\prime}_{g}\,x^{B^{\prime}_{g}}(1 -x)^{C^{\prime}_{g}} \tag{7.1}\] \[x\,u_{v}(x) =A_{u_{v}}\,x^{B_{u_{v}}}(1-x)^{C_{u_{v}}}\,(1+E_{u_{v}}x^{2})\] (7.2) \[x\,d_{v}(x) =A_{d_{v}}\,x^{B_{d_{v}}}(1-x)^{C_{d_{v}}}\] (7.3) \[x\,\bar{U}(x) =A_{\bar{U}}\,x^{B_{\bar{U}}}(1-x)^{C_{\bar{U}}}\,(1+D_{\bar{U}}x)\] (7.4) \[x\,\bar{D}(x) =A_{\bar{D}}\,x^{B_{\bar{D}}}(1-x)^{C_{\bar{D}}} \tag{7.5}\]

Then the normalization parameters \(A_{g}\), \(A_{u_{v}}\), \(A_{d_{v}}\) are fixed using the momentum sum rule and valence quark sum rules:

\[1 =\int\mathrm{d}x\,x\left(\sum_{i}(q_{i}(x)+\bar{q}_{i}(x))+g(x)\right) \tag{7.7}\] \[2 =\int\mathrm{d}x\,u_{v}(x)\] (7.8) \[1 =\int\mathrm{d}x\,d_{v}(x) \tag{7.9}\]

Other constraints are set on the parameters: \(B_{\bar{U}}=B_{\bar{D}}\), \(C^{\prime}_{g}=25\), \(A_{\bar{U}}=A_{\bar{D}}(1-f_{s})\) with \(f_{s}=0.4\). This leaves 14 free parameters in the fit.

#### 7.2.2 Theory

In order to calculate the theoretical predictions \(m^{i}\) for a given set of PDFs at specified order, one needs a pQCD calculation matching to the same order. In this analysis NLO pQCD theory implemented in the NLOJet++ code [92] is used to obtain the parton level jet cross sections. This code is using a variant of the Catani-Seymour subtraction [93] that relies onnumerical integration to integrate over the phase space. A PDF fit generally requires one to evaluate the theory prediction for many varying PDF inputs, which is impractical to do by repeating the slow integration each time. One way to improve the speed of the calculation is based on using the QCD factorization theorem [94]. In this method one represents the cross section as a sum of PDFs interpolated on a grid of discrete \(x\)-\(Q\) values and coupling \(\alpha_{s}(Q)\) interpolated on a grid of just the \(Q\) values with cross section kernels. The kernels are specific to a particular process, energy and jet observable, but can be calculated once (100 CPU days) and be quickly convoluted with an arbitrary PDF set (\(\ll 1\) CPU second) to get a result for the cross section. The FastNLO tables are generated for this measurement and are available at [95].

The partonic level jet cross section from the interpolated fixed order pQCD is further corrected to particle level using the multiplicative correction described in Section 6.6.1. The correction is assumed to be weakly dependent on the PDF set used.

#### 7.2.3 Definition of \(\chi^{2}\) in the fit

Equation 32 from [82] reads:

\[\chi^{2}_{\rm exp}=\sum_{i}\frac{[m^{i}-\sum_{j}\gamma^{i}_{j}m^{i}s_{j}-\mu^{ i}]^{2}}{\delta^{2}_{i,\rm stat}\mu^{i}m^{i}+\delta^{2}_{i,\rm uncor}(m^{i})^{2}}+ \sum_{j}s_{j}^{2}+\sum_{i}\log\frac{\delta^{2}_{i,\rm stat}\mu^{i}m^{i}+\delta ^{2}_{i,\rm uncor}(m^{i})^{2}}{(\delta^{2}_{i,\rm stat}+\delta^{2}_{i,\rm uncor })(\mu^{i})^{2}} \tag{7.11}\]

The first term includes a deviation in units of "sigma" of the theoretical prediction \(m^{i}\) from the measured data point \(\mu^{i}\). It is written under the assumption that the actual uncertainty has to be calculated from the true value \(\mu^{i}\), hence, the statistical uncertainty scales just as the Poisson variable \(\mu^{i}\sim\sqrt{N}\) and the uncorrelated uncertainty scales linearly, assuming a fixed relative uncertainty \(\delta_{i,\rm uncor}\). The \(s_{j}\) are so-called "nuisance parameters", each showing by how many "sigma" (towards the penalty accumulated in the second term) the relative correlated uncertainty vector \(\gamma^{i}_{j}\) is shifted. The third term with the logarithm originally introduced in [96] serves the purpose of minimising the bias associated with the presence of the variable quantity, such as \(m^{i}\) in the denominator of the first term (acting as the Gaussian width).

