EMC systematic uncertainty formula

A standard formula for evaluating the BEMC uncertainty on the measured jet that was used for previous jet spin asymmetry analyses in STAR looks like [111, 112]:

\[\frac{\delta\{p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{jet}}\}}{p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{jet}}}= \sqrt{R_{\mathrm{T}}^{2}\left(\frac{\delta\{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower}}\}}{ E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower}}}\right)^{2}+(1-R_{\mathrm{T}})^{2}\left(\frac{ \delta\{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{had.}}\}}{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{had.}}} \right)^{2}}\] (6.20 revisited)

where

\[\frac{\delta\{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower}}\}}{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower }}}\equiv\sqrt{\left(\frac{\delta\{\mathrm{gain}\}}{\mathrm{gain}}\right)^{2 }+\left(\frac{\delta\{\mathrm{tower\;eff.}\}}{\mathrm{tower\;eff.}}\right)^{2}}\] (6.21 revisited)

and

\[\frac{\delta\{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{had.}}\}}{E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{had.} }}\equiv\left(\frac{1}{\epsilon_{\mathrm{trk}}}S_{\mathrm{hadron}}-p_{ \mathrm{proj}}\right)\times f_{\mathrm{had.\,resp.}}\times\frac{\delta\{f_{ \mathrm{had.\,resp.}}\}}{f_{\mathrm{had.\,resp.}}}\] (6.22 revisited)

Equation (6.20) is not an entirely obvious one, especially the term defined in Eq. (6.22). A good way to get an understanding for it is to try to derive it.

### Proof

We start with a definition of measured \(p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{jet}}\) as a sum over its constituents:

\[p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{jet}}=\sum_{\mathrm{all}}p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{track }}+E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{tower}}-\Delta E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{sub.}}\] (E.4)where the contribution of hadrons is given by the energy deposition of photons coming from hadron decay and deposition from long lived neutral and charged hadrons:

\[E_{\rm T}^{\rm tower}=\left(\sum_{\gamma}+\sum_{\rm neut.\,had.}+\sum_{\rm chg. \,had.}\right)E_{\rm T}^{\rm tower}\] (E.5)

The other term, \(\Delta E_{\rm T}^{\rm sub.}\), represents the effect of the hadronic subtraction: towers that have tracks pointing to them have their energy reduced by the momentum of that track1, and if the resulting tower energy is negative the tower is discarded. In practice, the charged hadrons will often deposit only a fraction of their energy in the calorimeter. We also neglect the contributions from electrons (that leave tracks and deposit most of their energy in the calorimeter via an electromagnetic shower), because those are relatively rare to come by in our events. We also ignore contributions from photons that accidentally hit the same tower as a track. So for the purposes of this derivation we just assume that if a tower has a track pointing to it, that tower will be fully subtracted:

Footnote 1: This method does not take into account the situation when the deposited energy gets split between several towers.

\[\Delta E_{\rm T}^{\rm sub.}=\sum_{\rm proj.\,tower}E_{\rm T}^{\rm tower}\] (E.6)

The variables in Eq. (6.22) would need to be evaluated using the following definitions:

\[f_{\rm had.\,resp.}\equiv\frac{\left\langle\sum_{\rm proj.\,tower}E_{\rm T}^ {\rm tower}\right\rangle}{\left\langle\sum_{\rm proj.\,tower}p_{\rm T}^{\rm track }\right\rangle}\] (E.7)

to represent the fraction of energy that a charged hadron deposits in a calorimeter tower that its track projects to,

\[p_{\rm proj}\equiv\frac{\left\langle\sum_{\rm proj.\,tower}p_{\rm T}^{\rm track }\right\rangle}{\left\langle\sum_{\rm all}p_{\rm T}^{\rm track}\right\rangle}\] (E.8)to represent the fraction of tracks that point to at least one EMC tower,

\[S_{\text{hadron}}\equiv\frac{\left\langle\left(\sum_{\text{neut.\,had.}}+\sum_{ \text{chg.\,had.}}\right)E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\right\rangle}{\left\langle \sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\right\rangle}\] (E.9)

to parameterize an assumed scaling of neutral hadron energy with the charged hadron energy, and, finally,

\[\epsilon_{\text{trk}}\equiv\frac{\left\langle\sum_{\text{all}}p_{\text{T}}^{ \text{track}}\right\rangle}{\left\langle\sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}p_{\text{T}}^ {\text{track}}\right\rangle}\] (E.10)

to represent the tracking efficiency. In these four definitions, the \(\left\langle...\right\rangle\) denotes average of \(x\) in the events. One can notice that the definitions of the form

\[\frac{\left\langle\sum_{i}x\right\rangle_{\text{events}}}{\left\langle\sum_{ j}y\right\rangle_{\text{events}}}\equiv\frac{\sum_{\text{events}}\sum_{i}x}{\sum_{ \text{events}}\sum_{j}y}=\frac{\left\langle x\right\rangle_{\text{events},i}} {\left\langle y\right\rangle_{\text{events},j}}\] (E.11)

that were used in Eqs. (E.7) to (E.10) deviate from a more common form

\[\left\langle\frac{x}{y}\right\rangle_{\text{events}}\equiv\frac{1}{N_{\text{ events}}}\sum_{\text{events}}\frac{x}{y}.\] (E.12)

The definitions provided here allow to reduce the number of approximations that are needed for this proof. They are also similar to the ones that were used to determine the actual parameter values in [66].

Substituting Eqs. (E.5) and (E.6) into Eq. (E.4) one gets:

\[p_{\text{T}}^{\text{jet}}=\sum_{\text{all}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}}+\left( \sum_{\gamma}+\sum_{\text{neut.\,had.}}+\sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}-\sum_{\text{ proj.\,tower}}\right)E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\] (E.13)First, looking closer at the last term in the brackets

\[\begin{split}\sum_{\text{proj.\,tower}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}& \approx\frac{\left\langle\sum_{\text{proj.\,towers}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower }}\right\rangle\,\left\langle\sum_{\text{proj.\,towers}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track }}\right\rangle}{\left\langle\sum_{\text{proj.\,towers}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track }}\right\rangle\,\left\langle\sum_{\text{all}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}} \right\rangle}\sum_{\text{all}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}}=\\ &=f_{\text{had.\,resp.}}\,p_{\text{proj}}\sum_{\text{all}}p_{ \text{T}}^{\text{track}}\end{split}\] (E.14)

for which the following assumption had to be made:

\[\sum_{\text{proj.\,tower}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\approx\frac{\left\langle \sum_{\text{proj.\,tower}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\right\rangle}{\left\langle \sum_{\text{all}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}}\right\rangle}\sum_{\text{all}}p_ {\text{T}}^{\text{track}}.\] (E.15)

Second, looking at two other terms:

\[\begin{split}\left(\sum_{\text{neut.\,had.}}+\sum_{\text{chg. \,had.}}\right)E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\approx\\ &\approx\frac{\left\langle\left(\sum_{\text{neut.\,had.}}+\sum_{ \text{chg.\,had.}}\right)E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\right\rangle\,\frac{ \left\langle\sum_{\text{proj.\,towers}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\right\rangle }{\left\langle\sum_{\text{proj.\,towers}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}}\right\rangle }}{\left\langle\sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\right\rangle \,\frac{\left\langle\sum_{\text{all}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}}\right\rangle }{\left\langle\sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}}\right\rangle }}\sum_{\text{all}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}}=\\ &=S_{\text{hadron}}\frac{f_{\text{had.\,resp.}}}{\epsilon_{\text{ trk}}}\sum_{\text{all}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}}\end{split}\] (E.16)

for which three assumptions were used:

\[\sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\approx\frac{\left\langle \sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\right\rangle}{\left\langle \sum_{\text{all}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{track}}\right\rangle}\sum_{\text{all}}p_ {\text{T}}^{\text{track}},\] (E.17)

\[\sum_{\text{neut.\,had.}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\approx(S_{\text{hadron}}-1) \sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}}\] (E.18)

and

\[\frac{\left\langle\sum_{\text{proj.\,towers}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower}} \right\rangle}{\left\langle\sum_{\text{proj.\,towers}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{ track}}\right\rangle}\approx\frac{\left\langle\sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}E_{\text{T}}^{\text{tower }}\right\rangle}{\left\langle\sum_{\text{chg.\,had.}}p_{\text{T}}^{\text{ track}}\right\rangle}.\] (E.19)Substituting Eqs. (E.14) and (E.16) into Eq. (E.13) one arrives at an expression

\[p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\approx\sum_{\rm all}p_{\rm T}^{\rm track}+\sum_{\gamma}E_{ \rm T}^{\rm tower}+\left(\frac{S_{\rm hadron}}{\epsilon_{\rm trk}}-p_{\rm proj} \right)f_{\rm had.\,resp.}\sum_{\rm all}p_{\rm T}^{\rm track}.\] (E.20)

Finally, differentiating \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) by \(f_{\rm had.\,resp.}\) one sees the expression for the second term under the square root in Eq. (6.20):

\[\frac{\partial p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}}{\partial f_{\rm had.\,resp.}}\delta\{f_{ \rm had.\,resp.}\}=\left(\frac{S_{\rm hadron}}{\epsilon_{\rm trk}}-p_{\rm proj }\right)f_{\rm had.\,resp.}\frac{\delta\{f_{\rm had.\,resp.}\}}{f_{\rm had. \,resp.}}(1-R_{\rm T})\;p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\] (E.21)

### Discussion

Discrepancy in values of \(f_{\rm had.\,resp.}\) estimated from data and embedding need not necessarily result in uncertainty. One could imagine that it could be rectified by tuning parameters of the detector simulation responsible for the response to hadrons (e.g., lowering the hadronic transport cut [113] or switching from GCALOR to GHEISHA [114]). Another possibility is that it could be implemented as a correction to be applied to the simulation, although that may require to have information about particle-tower association that is not easily available in our current software framework.

